      "side": 1,
      "message": "This makes a little more sense.  But does this mean that f2fs_sync_file() syncs the filename only for new files?  I.e. recover_dentry() can never trigger later if links are created or deleted or if the filesystem is re-mounted?\n\nI\u0027m still a bit confused by the LOST_PINO flag.  It seems to be overloaded to mean two different things: either (a) the i_pino and i_name fields may be wrong, or (b) fsync\u0027ing the file will force a checkpoint.  But if f2fs_sync_file() only syncs the filename if the file is new, then why does it force a checkpoint whenever LOST_PINO?  Wouldn\u0027t it make more sense to use \u0027LOST_PINO \u0026\u0026 file is newly created\u0027, so that setting LOST_PINO doesn\u0027t cause unnecessary checkpoints?\n\nAlso, recover_dentry() should still check IS_ENCRYPTED() and IS_CASEFOLDED(), and return an error rather than crashing.",
